                        "sense_id": "747eb0b3-d930-3b88-9c70-583c645a4359",
                        "entry_id": "73672f52-0fe3-328e-81d8-c85654d70c9c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any of a group of flattened polygonal cells that together make up the pinacoderm in the dermal epithelium of sponges."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f3930289-bcc8-4b31-ade9-1ec6d3389dae",
                                "sense_id": "747eb0b3-d930-3b88-9c70-583c645a4359",
                                "text": "Minchin (1896, 1898, 1908) also concluded that the pinacocytes could leave the dermal epithelium and become calcoblasts in both larva and adult sponge."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d8a79c32-8b04-4f05-9745-65ca34564fa7",
                                "sense_id": "747eb0b3-d930-3b88-9c70-583c645a4359",
                                "text": "As you can see in Fig 27.7, sponges have only three types of cells in their bodies: pinacocytes (epithelial cells), choanocytes (collar cells) and amoebocytes (amoeboid cells)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "36228fbc-53d5-4ae7-8362-394614135513",
                                "sense_id": "747eb0b3-d930-3b88-9c70-583c645a4359",
                                "text": "Despite this, in some studies the origin of new pinacocytes is not clear or is disputed (Wilson and Penney, 1930; Borojevic and Lévi, 1965)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
